    <h3>Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis</h3>
    <p>I tested 9 configurations for AdaBoost, 18 for Gradient Boosting, and 27 for XGBoost to understand their impact on model performance:</p>
    
    <h4>Key Findings:</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>AdaBoost:</strong> Shows moderate variation (56.85% - 58.90%); best with n_estimators=100, lr=0.5</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient Boosting:</strong> Sensitive to depth; shallow trees (depth=3) performed better than deep trees</li>
      <li><strong>XGBoost:</strong> Sweet spot at n_estimators=50, lr=0.2, depth=5 (67.12%); deeper trees help with complex feature interactions</li>
      <li><strong>Learning Rate Impact:</strong> XGBoost benefits from higher learning rates (0.2) with moderate tree counts</li>
      <li><strong>Overfitting Control:</strong> XGBoost's regularization allows deeper trees without overfitting</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>Model Comparison Summary</h3>
    <p><strong>Enhanced Features Unlock Different Algorithm Strengths:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>XGBoost (67.12%):</strong> Best performer - regularization and advanced splitting handle complex economy/state interactions</li>
      <li><strong>AdaBoost (58.90%):</strong> Modest improvement - sample reweighting less effective with state features</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient Boosting (56.16%):</strong> Slightly below baseline - may need more tuning for state-based features</li>
    </ul>
    
    <p><strong>Why XGBoost Wins with Enhanced Features:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Better Feature Interactions:</strong> L1/L2 regularization handles correlated features (equipment, player counts)</li>
      <li><strong>Optimal Tree Pruning:</strong> Depth-based pruning captures economy × time interactions without overfitting</li>
      <li><strong>Built-in Missing Value Handling:</strong> Gracefully handles missing values in time-series samples</li>
      <li><strong>Gradient-Based Optimization:</strong> More efficient at learning from state-based features</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>Comparison with Previous Methods</h3>
    <div class="classification-report">
      <h4>Method Performance on CS2 Round Prediction:</h4>
      <pre>Method                           Accuracy    Improvement
------------------------------------------------
Decision Trees (baseline)        50-57%      -
Naive Bayes (baseline)          52-54%      -
Boosting (old features)         58.70%      baseline
------------------------------------------------
XGBoost (enhanced features)     67.12%      +8.42pp 🎯
AdaBoost (enhanced features)    58.90%      +0.20pp
Gradient Boost (enhanced)       56.16%      -2.54pp

Best Performer: XGBoost with Enhanced State-Based Features</pre>
    </div>
    
    <p><strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The combination of state-based time-series features AND XGBoost's advanced gradient boosting produces a significant accuracy improvement. This demonstrates that <strong>feature engineering is more important than algorithm choice</strong> - enhanced features improved XGBoost by 8.42pp, but barely helped AdaBoost.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>(e) Conclusions</h2>
    <p>The boosting methods were able to predict the winner of a round with 67.12% accuracy. The extreme gradient boosting was the best model by performance and shows the importance of trying multiple models.</p>
    
    <p>The most critical insight for CS2 players is that <strong>economy dominates round outcomes</strong>—equipment advantage emerged as the #1 predictor of round winners, far more important than tactical objectives like bomb plant status. This quantitatively confirms what experienced players know intuitively: teams with more money to buy superior weapons (AK47s, AWPs, M4A1s) have dramatically higher win probability than teams forced to eco with pistols. By sampling game state at 10 different time points throughout each round rather than just at the end, the enhanced features captured the temporal dynamics of how player counts, health, and equipment interact to determine outcomes. The 8.42 percentage point improvement from state-based features over outcome-based features demonstrates that feature engineering matters more than algorithm sophistication—what you measure is more important than how you model it. For players, this analysis reveals that winning the economy war (forcing opponent eco rounds, preserving weapons, strategic saving) is the single most controllable factor influencing match outcomes, and teams should prioritize economic decision-making over pure mechanical skill execution when strategizing for competitive play.</p>
  </div>

